# Express API server configuration.
# Copy to server/.env — or export these in your shell — before `npm run dev`.
#
# Every variable the API reads is listed here, in the same order as the full
# reference in DEPLOY.md §6 (which also documents exactly what happens when
# one is left unset). Commented-out lines are the optional ones.

# Port the API listens on (frontend expects 4000 by default).
PORT=4000

# Comma-separated allowed CORS origins. Any localhost / 127.0.0.1 /
# *.localhost origin is always allowed on top of this, so it only matters
# once there's a real domain — where it is REQUIRED, or the browser is
# refused on every call.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Where the two halves live. Used for Stripe return URLs and for every link
# the server puts in an email or a bell notification (WEB_URL), and for the
# email open pixel + unsubscribe links (API_URL). Defaults are the localhost
# dev ports — set both for real, or emailed links point at the RECIPIENT's
# own machine.
# WEB_URL=https://app.activityos.uk
# API_URL=https://api.activityos.uk
#
# Invoice pay-link emails read their own var (routes/invoices.ts) instead of
# WEB_URL. Set it to the same value until that's unified.
# PUBLIC_WEB_URL=https://app.activityos.uk
# APP_URL=https://app.activityos.uk

# Express "trust proxy". The public rate limits key on the client IP, which
# behind a proxy is the proxy's unless this is right. Default: 1 hop in
# production (correct for Railway/Render/Vercel), off otherwise. A hop count,
# or the literal "false".
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Serve the interactive API docs (/docs, /openapi.json) in production too.
# They are always on outside production; off in production because the whole
# API surface is a map for an attacker.
# PUBLIC_API_DOCS=1

# Firebase Admin credentials — EITHER:
#   1. save your service-account JSON as server/serviceAccountKey.json
#      (Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new
#      private key), which is gitignored, OR
#   2. point this at the JSON file:
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/serviceAccountKey.json
#      OR
#   3. paste the JSON itself here — raw, or base64 so newlines survive a
#      host's secret store (`base64 -i serviceAccountKey.json`). This is how
#      a hosted API (Railway/Render/Fly) gets its credentials.
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
#
# With none of these (and no emulator) the API throws on startup.

# Platform (HQ) super-admin — the API creates this account automatically on
# startup when both vars are set (idempotent; an existing admin's password is
# never touched). Use a TEMP password; the admin sets their own via
# "Forgot password?" on the login page after first sign-in.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-please

# Transactional email (booking confirmations, payment links…). Any SMTP
# provider works — Resend, Mailgun, SendGrid, a Gmail app password, etc.
# LEAVE UNSET in development: emails then go to a throwaway Ethereal inbox
# and every send logs a clickable preview URL in the API console.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=resend
# SMTP_PASS=your-api-key
# MAIL_FROM="APF Activity Camps <bookings@yourdomain.com>"

# Mail only actually goes to real recipients when MAIL_LIVE=1. This is
# deliberately NOT implied by NODE_ENV: every host sets NODE_ENV=production,
# so the first staging deploy would otherwise start emailing real parents
# from the scheduler sweeps. Without it, only MAIL_ALLOWLIST addresses
# receive anything and everything else is logged, skipped and reported as
# suppressed. The API's startup log says which mode is active.
# MAIL_LIVE=1
# MAIL_ALLOWLIST=you@example.com, colleague@example.com

# Send as <tenant-slug>@<your domain> rather than the single MAIL_FROM local
# part. CANNOT work on Gmail SMTP (it rewrites From to the authenticated
# account) — only turn on with an ESP that does domain authentication.
# MAIL_PER_TENANT_FROM=1

# Only needed when running against the Firebase emulators instead of a real
# project (no credentials required then):
# FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:8080
# FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=demo-activityos

# AI assistant (the "AI assistant" view in every portal). Answers questions
# from the account's live data via Groq — key stays server-side. Unset =
# the view explains the assistant isn't configured.
# Get a key at https://console.groq.com/keys
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your-key
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b

# Stripe. The PLATFORM account's secret key: parents' payments are direct
# charges on each provider's connected Express account, so this key only
# orchestrates. Unset = every payment, pay-link, Connect-onboarding and
# subscription route is dead.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-key

# Stripe Billing webhook (plan subscriptions — separate from Stripe Connect).
# Local dev: stripe listen --forward-to localhost:4000/api/stripe/webhook
# and paste the printed whsec_… here. Without it the 6-hourly
# subscription-sync sweep still keeps statuses truthful, just slower.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-secret
#
# A SECOND accepted signing secret, for when Connect events are configured as
# their own Stripe endpoint. Both are tried before a signature is rejected.
# STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-other-secret
#
# DEV ONLY: let a provider who hasn't finished Express onboarding take
# payments on the PLATFORM account (flagged on the payment record) so payment
# testing isn't blocked. REMOVE IN PRODUCTION — unonboarded providers must
# not take money.
# STRIPE_PLATFORM_FALLBACK=1

# ── Inbound email (parent replies land in the in-app Inbox) ────────────────
# A provider forwards their own mailbox to <slug>@INBOUND_EMAIL_DOMAIN and the
# mail shows up in Email → Inbox. Unset = the whole feature hides itself: the
# "connect your mailbox" panel won't render and no address is handed out.
#
# The domain must actually receive mail. Cheapest route is a Resend-managed
# receiving domain (Emails → Receiving → ⋯ → Receiving address), which is free,
# needs no DNS, and catches every address at it. A custom subdomain works too,
# with its MX pointed at your inbound provider.
# INBOUND_EMAIL_DOMAIN=your-id.resend.app
#
# Shared secret for POST /api/emails/inbound — the generic endpoint used by
# curl and the e2e suite. Falls back to "dev-inbound" when unset, which is fine
# locally and MUST be set anywhere the API is publicly reachable.
# INBOUND_EMAIL_SECRET=some-long-random-string

# Resend inbound webhook (POST /api/emails/inbound/resend). Only needed to
# receive REAL mail; simulating an inbound message with curl needs neither.
# The API key fetches the message body (Resend's webhook carries metadata
# only) — the same key as SMTP_PASS when you send through Resend. The webhook
# secret verifies the Svix signature: Resend → Webhooks → add your public URL
# + the `email.received` event, then paste the whsec_… it shows you.
# Local dev needs a public URL, e.g. ngrok http 4000 or cloudflared tunnel.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_your-key
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-secret

# Signs private image links (children's photos, injury photos) and unsubscribe
# links. REQUIRED in production — generate once with `openssl rand -hex 32` and
# never rotate casually (every emailed unsubscribe link would stop working).
# Locally it can be left out: the key is derived from the service account.
# URL_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ── Optional integrations ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Address search + map tiles via Ordnance Survey (server-side, so no map key
# ever reaches the browser). Unset = OpenStreetMap/Nominatim, which is
# keyless but rate-limited: fine for dev, not for production traffic.
# OS_API_KEY=

# Live Google reviews via the Places API (each tenant also supplies its Place
# ID). Unset = the Google source is simply absent from the reviews board.
# GOOGLE_PLACES_KEY=

# Trustpilot service reviews (each tenant also supplies its Business Unit ID).
# Unset = the Trustpilot source is absent.
# TRUSTPILOT_API_KEY=

# "Connect your Google Business Profile" OAuth. Both are needed; without them
# the connect endpoint answers 501 and the UI shows it as unavailable.
# GOOGLE_BP_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_BP_REDIRECT=https://api.activityos.uk/api/reviews/google/callback
